18	 *  1. Upgrade proration priced the RAW list-price monthly delta while the original
19	 *     order applied both the term discount and the seasonal promo. On a 36-month
20	 *     term with the 30% Summer Sale running, upgrading Starter -> Max the day after
21	 *     purchase cost $1,044.45 against $665.08 for buying Max outright on the same
22	 *     end date: the customer overpaid $379.37.
23	 *  2. The downgrade CREDIT used that same undiscounted delta over an UNCLAMPED
24	 *     remaining term, so it refunded more than had ever been paid — a self-service
25	 *     money printer that was one cheap-but-storage-heavy plan away from reachable
26	 *     (blocked today only by the disk-monotonic guard, QA P1-14).
27	 *
28	 * Both directions now price through lib/proration.ts. These tests hold the two
29	 * halves against each other so they cannot drift apart again.
30	 *
31	 * Deterministic: every `now` and every promo is injected — nothing reads the clock
32	 * except through a value supplied by the test.
